# textbook_em_dl.py
# Deep, comprehensive point-by-point ChatGPT style tutorials for all 8 chapters of EM & DL

CHAPTERS_EM_DL = {('EM', 0): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Discrete Mathematics - Mathematical Logic & Set Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Strict Courtroom Contract Clause', 'text': "• Proposition: Like entering a Wi-Fi password—it is strictly True or False. Sentences like 'What time is it?' are not propositions.\n• Implication (P -> Q): Dad promises 'If you score AIR 1 (P), I will buy you a bike (Q)'. He only breaks his word if you get AIR 1 and he refuses to buy the bike (T -> F = False). If you do not get AIR 1, whatever happens never violates the promise (Vacuous Truth)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '1.1', 'title': 'Propositions & The 5 Core Logic Connectives (Complete Truth Table)', 'content': "• <b>What is a Proposition?</b> A declarative sentence that is either strictly True (T) or strictly False (F), but never both.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- '5 is a prime number' is a proposition (True).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 'Please close the door' is NOT a proposition (command).<br/><br/>• <b>Complete Master Truth Table for the 5 Operators:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>P   Q   ~P (NOT)   P ∧ Q (AND)   P ∨ Q (OR)   P → Q (Implication)   P ↔ Q (Biconditional)<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>T   T      F           T             T                 T                       T<br/>T   F      F           F             T                 F (Broken promise!)     F<br/>F   T      T           F             T                 T (Vacuously true)      F<br/>F   F      T           F             F                 T (Vacuously true)      T</div>• <b>Crucial Implication Identity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>(P → Q) ≡ (~P ∨ Q)</code> (Use this identity to rewrite implications in 99% of GATE proofs!).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is False ONLY when P = True and Q = False! When P is False, (P → Q) is 100% GUARANTEED to be True regardless of Q (Vacuous Truth).'}, {'number': '1.2', 'title': 'Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse', 'content': "• For any conditional statement <b>P → Q</b> ('If P, then Q'):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> 'If NOT Q, then NOT P'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>100% Logically Equivalent:</b> <code>(P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Proving the contrapositive proves the original statement!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> 'If Q, then P'. NOT logically equivalent to P → Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> 'If NOT P, then NOT Q'. Equivalent to the Converse, NOT the original.<br/><br/>• <b>Example:</b> 'If it rains (P), the grass is wet (Q)':<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Contrapositive: 'If the grass is NOT wet (~Q), it did NOT rain (~P)' (True!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Converse: 'If grass is wet, it rained' (False! A sprinkler could have caused it).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Converse (Q → P) and Inverse (~P → ~Q) are logically equivalent to EACH OTHER, but NEVER to the original statement!'}, {'number': '1.3', 'title': "First-Order Logic: Quantifiers (∀ vs ∃) & De Morgan's Rules", 'content': "• <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> 'For all x'. True only if P(x) holds for EVERY single element in the universe.<br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> 'There exists an x'. True if at least ONE element makes P(x) true.<br/><br/>• <b>De Morgan's Negation Rules for Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>~[ ∀x P(x) ] ≡ ∃x [ ~P(x) ]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Negation of 'All students passed' is 'There is at least one student who did NOT pass'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <code>~[ ∃x P(x) ] ≡ ∀x [ ~P(x) ]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Negation of 'Someone scored 100' is 'Everyone scored not 100'.<br/><br/>• <b>Order of Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>∀x ∃y P(x, y)</code> (Everyone loves someone) is NOT equal to <code>∃y ∀x P(x, y)</code> (There is one specific person whom everybody loves!).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Negating 'All X are Y' is 'There exists an X that is NOT Y'! It is NEVER 'No X are Y'. Negating ∀ gives ∃, and negating ∃ gives ∀."}, {'number': '1.4', 'title': 'Relations & Properties (Counting Formulas for Set of Size n)', 'content': "• Let set A contain <b>n</b> elements. The Cartesian product A × A contains <b>n²</b> ordered pairs.<br/>• <b>The 5 Fundamental Counting Formulas to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>1. Total Relations:               2^(n²)<br/>2. Reflexive Relations:           2^(n² - n)<br/>3. Symmetric Relations:           2^(n(n + 1) / 2)<br/>4. Reflexive & Symmetric:         2^(n(n - 1) / 2)<br/>5. Equivalence Relations:         Bell Numbers B(n): B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15, B(5)=52</div>• <b>Equivalence Relation Definition:</b> A relation that is simultaneously <b>Reflexive</b>, <b>Symmetric</b>, and <b>Transitive</b>. It partitions the set into disjoint Equivalence Classes.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a set with 4 elements, total equivalence relations = Bell Number B(4) = 15! Reflexive relations = 2^(16 - 4) = 2^12 = 4096.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Counting Symmetric Relations', 'problem': 'Set A has 4 elements: A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. How many symmetric relations can be formed on set A?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for symmetric relations on a set of size n is: Total = 2^(n(n+1)/2).', 'Step 2: Here n = 4.', 'Step 3: Exponent = (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.', 'Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.'], 'answer': 'Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.'}], 'exam_traps': ['A relation can be BOTH symmetric and anti-symmetric at the same time (e.g. Identity relation on any set)!', 'Empty relation on a non-empty set is NOT reflexive, but it is vacuously symmetric and transitive!']}, ('EM', 1): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Combinatorics, Recurrence Relations & Graph Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Sock Drawer in the Dark & Party Handshakes', 'text': '• Pigeonhole Principle: If a drawer has black and brown socks, pulling 3 socks guarantees at least one matching pair because 3 socks go into 2 color buckets.\n• Handshaking Theorem: At any party, every handshake involves 2 people. So if you sum all individual handshakes counted by each person, the sum is ALWAYS an even number!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '2.1', 'title': 'Pigeonhole Principle (PHP) & Guaranteed Matches', 'content': '• <b>Basic Pigeonhole Principle:</b> If <code>k + 1</code> or more objects are placed into <code>k</code> boxes, then at least one box must contain 2 or more objects.<br/>• <b>Generalized PHP:</b> If <code>N</code> objects are placed into <code>k</code> boxes, at least one box contains at least <b>⌈N / k⌉</b> objects.<br/>• <b>Inverse PHP Question (Common in GATE):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;To guarantee that at least <b>m</b> items land in the same box out of <b>k</b> boxes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Minimum Items Needed = k · (m - 1) + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Example:</i> To guarantee 5 cards of the same suit (4 suits): 4 · (5 - 1) + 1 = 17 cards.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In guaranteed match questions: Worst-case scenario is having (m - 1) items in every single box. Adding 1 more item triggers the guarantee: N_min = k(m - 1) + 1!'}, {'number': '2.2', 'title': 'Graph Theory: Handshaking Theorem & Planar Graphs', 'content': "• <b>Handshaking Lemma:</b> In any undirected graph: <b>∑ deg(v) = 2|E|</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Corollary:</i> In every graph, the number of vertices with ODD degree is ALWAYS EVEN!<br/>• <b>Complete Graph (K_n):</b> Total edges = <b>n(n - 1) / 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Bipartite Graph:</b> Graph is bipartite IF AND ONLY IF it has <b>NO odd-length cycles</b> (2-colorable).<br/><br/>• <b>Planar Graphs & Euler's Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>V - E + R = 2</b> (where R is total regions/faces including the infinite exterior face).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Maximum edges in any simple connected planar graph: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b> (for V ≥ 3).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If triangle-free (no C_3 cycle): <b>E ≤ 2V - 4</b>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: K_5 (complete graph on 5 vertices) and K_{3,3} (complete bipartite utility graph) are the two minimal non-planar graphs (Kuratowski's Theorem)!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Planar Graph Faces Calculation', 'problem': 'A connected planar graph has 8 vertices and each vertex has degree 3. How many faces (regions) does it divide the plane into?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate total edges using Handshaking Theorem: ∑ deg(v) = 2E.', 'Step 2: 8 vertices with degree 3 ⇒ Total degree sum = 8 × 3 = 24.', 'Step 3: 2E = 24 ⇒ E = 12 edges.', "Step 4: Use Euler's planar formula: V - E + R = 2.", 'Step 5: Substitute V = 8, E = 12: 8 - 12 + R = 2 ⇒ -4 + R = 2 ⇒ R = 6.'], 'answer': 'Total regions R = 6 (5 bounded faces + 1 unbounded outer face).'}], 'exam_traps': ["Euler's formula V - E + R = 2 requires the graph to be CONNECTED. If disconnected with k components: V - E + R = 1 + k.", 'Sum of degrees in any graph is ALWAYS an even number. If an exam question presents degree sequence with an odd sum, such a graph CANNOT exist!']}, ('EM', 2): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Linear Algebra - Matrices, Systems of Equations & Eigenvalues', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: 3D Video Game Graphics & Bridge Resonance', 'text': '• Matrices: In 3D games, moving and rotating a character is simply multiplying 3D coordinate vectors by 4x4 transformation matrices.\n• Eigenvalues: The natural resonance frequencies of a bridge or skyscraper. If earthquake vibrations match an eigenvalue frequency, catastrophic collapse happens!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '3.1', 'title': 'System of Linear Equations (AX = B) Consistency', 'content': '• Consider system <b>AX = B</b> with n variables. Let [A | B] be the augmented matrix.<br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Consistency Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Inconsistent (No Solution):</b> <code>Rank(A) ≠ Rank(A | B)</code> (i.e. Rank(A) < Rank(A | B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Unique Solution:</b> <code>Rank(A) == Rank(A | B) == n</code> (Full rank equal to number of unknowns).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Infinitely Many Solutions:</b> <code>Rank(A) == Rank(A | B) = r < n</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of linearly independent free variables = <b>n - r</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Homogeneous System (AX = 0):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Always consistent (trivial solution X = 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Has non-zero (non-trivial) solutions IF AND ONLY IF <b>det(A) = 0</b> (Rank(A) < n).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Homogeneous system AX = 0 has non-trivial solutions IF AND ONLY IF det(A) = 0. For non-homogeneous AX = B, check Rank(A) vs Rank(A|B)!'}, {'number': '3.2', 'title': 'Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors: The 5 Golden Properties', 'content': '• Characteristic Equation: <code>det(A - λI) = 0</code>.<br/>• <b>The 5 Golden Properties for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sum of Eigenvalues = Trace of A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements): <code>∑ λ_i = Tr(A)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Product of Eigenvalues = Determinant of A</b>: <code>∏ λ_i = det(A)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Triangular / Diagonal Matrices:</b> Eigenvalues are SIMPLY the main diagonal entries!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If λ is an eigenvalue of A:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^k has eigenvalue <b>λ^k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^(-1) has eigenvalue <b>1 / λ</b> (provided λ ≠ 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- (A + cI) has eigenvalue <b>λ + c</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Real Symmetric Matrices:</b> Always have strictly REAL eigenvalues.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 90% of GATE eigenvalue questions, DO NOT solve the cubic equation! Just verify Trace = Sum(λ) and Det = Product(λ) against the 4 options to find the answer in 10 seconds!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Eigenvalues using Trace and Det', 'problem': 'Find the eigenvalues of A = [[3, 2], [1, 4]].', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate Trace(A) = sum of main diagonal = 3 + 4 = 7.', 'Step 2: Calculate Det(A) = (3 × 4) - (2 × 1) = 12 - 2 = 10.', 'Step 3: Sum of eigenvalues λ1 + λ2 = 7 and Product λ1 · λ2 = 10.', 'Step 4: Two numbers that add to 7 and multiply to 10 are 2 and 5.'], 'answer': 'Eigenvalues are λ1 = 2, λ2 = 5.'}], 'exam_traps': ['det(A + B) is NOT det(A) + det(B)! However, det(A × B) = det(A) × det(B).', 'Scaling an n × n matrix by constant k scales determinant by k^n: det(k·A) = k^n · det(A)!']}, ('EM', 3): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': "Calculus, Probability Distributions & Bayes' Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Analogy: The Rare Disease Medical Test (Bayes' Paradox)", 'text': "• Bayes' Theorem: A medical test is 99% accurate, but a disease affects only 1 in 10,000 people. If you test positive, what is the chance you actually have the disease? Surprisingly, only ~1%! Because false positives from the 9,999 healthy people vastly outnumber true positives from the 1 sick person."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '4.1', 'title': "Bayes' Theorem & Conditional Probability", 'content': "• <b>Conditional Probability Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b> Events A and B are independent if and only if <b>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Bayes' Rule Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A_i | B) = [ P(B | A_i) · P(A_i) ] / ∑ [ P(B | A_j) · P(A_j) ]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Where the denominator is the Law of Total Probability P(B).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Mutually exclusive means they cannot occur together (P(A ∩ B) = 0). Independent means occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. They are completely different concepts!'}, {'number': '4.2', 'title': 'Probability Distributions: Binomial, Poisson & Normal', 'content': '• <b>1. Binomial Distribution B(n, p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- P(X = k) = C(n, k) · p^k · (1 - p)^{n - k}.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>n · p</b>; Variance = <b>n · p · (1 - p)</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>2. Poisson Distribution (Rate λ):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Models rare events: P(X = k) = (e^(-λ) · λ^k) / k!.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Unique Property:</b> Mean = <b>λ</b> and Variance = <b>λ</b> (Mean == Variance!).<br/><br/>• <b>3. Uniform Distribution on [a, b]:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>(a + b) / 2</b>; Variance = <b>(b - a)² / 12</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Poisson distribution, Mean = Variance = λ! If a question states 'the variance of a Poisson variable is 9', then immediately mean = 9 and λ = 9!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Bayes' Theorem in Factory Production", 'problem': 'Factory A produces 70% of items with 1% defect rate. Factory B produces 30% of items with 5% defect rate. An item chosen at random is defective. What is the probability it was made by Factory B?', 'steps': ['Step 1: P(A) = 0.70, P(B) = 0.30.', 'Step 2: P(D | A) = 0.01, P(D | B) = 0.05.', 'Step 3: Total Probability P(D) = (0.70 × 0.01) + (0.30 × 0.05) = 0.007 + 0.015 = 0.022.', "Step 4: By Bayes' Theorem: P(B | D) = [ P(B) · P(D | B) ] / P(D) = 0.015 / 0.022 = 15 / 22 ≈ 0.6818."], 'answer': 'P(Factory B | Defective) = 15 / 22 (approx 68.18%).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Variance can NEVER be negative! Var(X) = E[X²] - (E[X])² ≥ 0.', 'Scaling rule: Var(aX + b) = a² · Var(X). The constant b adds 0 to variance!']}, ('DL', 0): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Number Systems, Signed Numbers & IEEE-754 Floating Point', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Odometer Rollback & Game High Score Overflow', 'text': "• 2's Complement: Like a car odometer rolling backward from 00000 to 99999. In an 8-bit binary computer, rolling backward from 00000000 gives 11111111, which represents -1!\n• Overflow: In early arcade games (like Pac-Man level 256), adding 1 to 255 caused the 8-bit byte to overflow back to 0, breaking the game."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '5.1', 'title': "Signed Number Ranges & 2's Complement Arithmetic", 'content': "• <b>For an n-bit binary representation:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Representation    Range for n bits                Number of Zeros   Special Notes<br/>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Unsigned          0 to 2^n - 1                    1 (00...0)        All bits represent magnitude<br/>Sign-Magnitude    -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1) 2 (+0 and -0)    MSB is sign bit<br/>1's Complement    -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1) 2 (+0 and -0)    Bitwise inversion<br/>2's Complement    -2^(n-1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)      1 (Unique zero!)  Standard in all modern CPUs</div>• <b>Asymmetry in 2's Complement:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For n = 8 bits: Range is <b>-128 to +127</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Notice that <b>-128 has NO positive counterpart</b> in 8 bits! Attempting to negate -128 causes integer overflow.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 2's complement with n bits: The minimum negative number is -2^(n-1). It has no positive counterpart! Fast 2's complement shortcut: Copy bits from right to left up to and including the first '1', then flip all remaining bits to the left!"}, {'number': '5.2', 'title': 'IEEE-754 Single Precision 32-bit Floating Point', 'content': "• <b>Bit Fields: [ Sign (1 bit) | Biased Exponent E (8 bits) | Mantissa M (23 bits) ]</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Field          Bit Count   Function<br/>--------------------------------------------------------------------------<br/>Sign (S)       1 bit       0 for Positive, 1 for Negative<br/>Exponent (E)   8 bits      Excess-127 Bias: Stored E = actual exponent e + 127<br/>Mantissa (M)   23 bits     Fractional part with implicit leading 1: 1.M</div>• <b>Value Formula:</b> <code>(-1)^S × (1.M) × 2^(E - 127)</code>.<br/>• <b>Special Patterns:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 255 and M = 0: Represents <b>±Infinity</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 255 and M ≠ 0: Represents <b>NaN (Not a Number)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If E = 0 and M = 0: Represents <b>±0</b>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The leading '1' in normalized floating point (1.M) is NEVER stored in memory—it is implicit! This gives 24 bits of precision from 23 stored mantissa bits."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: IEEE-754 Representation of -13.625', 'problem': 'Convert -13.625 into IEEE-754 32-bit hexadecimal format.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Sign is negative ⇒ S = 1.', 'Step 2: 13.625 in binary: 13 = 1101; 0.625 = 0.101 ⇒ 1101.101.', 'Step 3: Normalize: 1101.101 = 1.101101 × 2^3.', 'Step 4: Actual exponent e = 3 ⇒ Stored E = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010 in binary.', 'Step 5: Mantissa M = 101101 followed by 17 zeros.', 'Step 6: Combine: 1 | 10000010 | 10110100000000000000000. In Hex: 0xC15A0000.'], 'answer': 'Hexadecimal value = 0xC15A0000.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In IEEE-754, the smallest positive normalized number is 1.0 × 2^(-126) (where E = 1, not E = 0!).', 'Double precision IEEE-754 uses 64 bits: 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits (bias = 1023), and 52 mantissa bits.']}, ('DL', 1): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Boolean Algebra, Logic Gates & K-Map Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Universal LEGO Brick & Map Minimizer', 'text': '• Universal Gates: Just as LEGO bricks can build any spaceship, NAND and NOR gates alone can build any computer circuit.\n• K-Map: A 2D puzzle where adjacent boxes differ by only 1 bit. Grouping 1s cancels out changing variables, shrinking circuit size.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '6.1', 'title': 'Boolean Laws & The Consensus Theorem', 'content': "• <b>Consensus Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Variable A appears in true form and complemented form (A, A').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The term <b>BC</b> is redundant (consensus) and can be completely deleted!<br/><br/>• <b>Minimum Gate Counts for Universal Gates:</b><br/><div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>Target Gate   NAND Gates Required   NOR Gates Required<br/>-------------------------------------------------------<br/>NOT                  1                     1<br/>AND                  2                     3<br/>OR                   3                     2<br/>XOR                  4                     5<br/>XNOR                 5                     4</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: XOR requires 4 NAND gates (or 5 NOR gates). XNOR requires 5 NAND gates (or 4 NOR gates). Notice the perfect reciprocal symmetry: 4 vs 5!'}, {'number': '6.2', 'title': 'Karnaugh Maps (K-Maps), Prime & Essential Prime Implicants', 'content': "• <b>Implicant:</b> Any product term that covers one or more 1s.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangular group of size 2^k (1, 2, 4, 8) that CANNOT be merged into a larger group.<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that covers at least one '1' that is NOT covered by any other prime implicant.<br/>• <b>Minimal SOP Formula:</b> MUST include ALL Essential Prime Implicants!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every EPI is a PI, but not every PI is an EPI! To find EPIs fast: look for a '1' that belongs to only ONE valid largest rectangle."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Boolean Function Minimization', 'problem': "Simplify F = AB + A'C + BC using Boolean laws.", 'steps': ["Step 1: Check terms: AB, A'C, BC.", "Step 2: Variable A appears complemented (A and A').", 'Step 3: The third term BC is the product of remaining variables.', 'Step 4: By Consensus Theorem, BC is redundant and can be deleted.'], 'answer': "Simplified function: F = AB + A'C."}], 'exam_traps': ["In K-maps, grouping 0s gives the minimized POS expression (F'), not SOP!", "Don't care terms (X) can be included to enlarge groups, but you are NEVER required to cover all don't cares."]}, ('DL', 2): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Combinational Circuits - Multiplexers, Decoders & Adders', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Train Track Switch & Letter Mail Sorter', 'text': '• Multiplexer (MUX): Like a railway switch choosing which incoming track connects to the single outgoing main line, controlled by switch levers (select lines).\n• Decoder: A mail sorter reading a zip code and directing the parcel to exactly one specific delivery box.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '7.1', 'title': 'Multiplexer (MUX) as a Universal Logic Module', 'content': "• A <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b> has 2^n data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>Function Implementation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Using a <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b>: Connect all n variables directly to select lines. Connect truth table outputs (0 or 1) to data inputs. No extra gates needed!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Using a <b>2^(n-1) : 1 MUX</b>: Connect (n - 1) variables to select lines. The last variable X can provide data inputs as <b>0, 1, X, or X'</b>. May need an inverter for X'.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any Boolean function of n variables can be implemented using a single 2^(n-1) : 1 MUX with at most one external NOT gate!'}, {'number': '7.2', 'title': 'Half Adder vs Full Adder Architecture', 'content': '• <b>Half Adder (HA):</b> Adds 2 bits A and B.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <code>A ⊕ B</code>; Carry = <code>A · B</code>.<br/>• <b>Full Adder (FA):</b> Adds 3 bits: A, B, and Carry-in C_in.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <code>A ⊕ B ⊕ C_in</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry-out = <code>AB + C_in(A ⊕ B)</code>.<br/>• <b>Hardware Construction:</b> 1 Full Adder = <b>2 Half Adders + 1 OR gate</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To build an n-bit Ripple Carry Adder, you need 1 Half Adder and (n - 1) Full Adders. Total delay = n × (carry propagation delay).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Implementing Logic with 4:1 MUX', 'problem': 'Implement F(A, B, C) = ∑m(1, 2, 4, 7) using a 4:1 MUX with select lines S1 = A, S0 = B.', 'steps': ['Step 1: For AB = 00: m0(C=0)->0, m1(C=1)->1 ⇒ I0 = C.', "Step 2: For AB = 01: m2(C=0)->1, m3(C=1)->0 ⇒ I1 = C'.", "Step 3: For AB = 10: m4(C=0)->1, m5(C=1)->0 ⇒ I2 = C'.", 'Step 4: For AB = 11: m6(C=0)->0, m7(C=1)->1 ⇒ I3 = C.'], 'answer': "Inputs to 4:1 MUX: I0 = C, I1 = C', I2 = C', I3 = C."}], 'exam_traps': ['Pay strict attention to MSB vs LSB on select lines (S1 = A, S0 = B vs S1 = B, S0 = A)!', 'Active-low enables in decoders require invert logic before cascading.']}, ('DL', 3): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Sequential Circuits, Flip-Flops & Counters', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: High-Speed Camera Snapshot & Subway Turnstile', 'text': "• Flip-Flop: A high-speed camera taking a snapshot strictly on the rising edge of the photographer's flash. Inputs must stay stable before (setup time) and after (hold time) the flash.\n• Counter: A subway turnstile clicker that increments each time a passenger passes through, cycling back to zero when reaching its maximum count."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '8.1', 'title': 'Flip-Flop Characteristic Equations', 'content': "• <b>JK Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = J·Q' + K'·Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=0: Hold (Q)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=1: Reset (0)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=0: Set (1)<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=1: Toggle (Q') - eliminates SR invalid state!<br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = D</code> (data storage).<br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop:</b> <code>Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q</code> (T=0: Hold, T=1: Toggle).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The characteristic equations must be on your fingertips: JK: Q_{next} = J·Q' + K'·Q | D: Q_{next} = D | T: Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q."}, {'number': '8.2', 'title': 'Setup Time, Hold Time & Max Clock Frequency', 'content': '• <b>Setup Time (t_setup):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable BEFORE active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Hold Time (t_hold):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable AFTER active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Minimum Clock Period (T_clk):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_clk ≥ t_{cq} + t_{comb} + t_{setup}</b>.<br/>• <b>Maximum Clock Frequency:</b> <code>f_max = 1 / T_clk_min</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To avoid Hold time violations: t_{cq_min} + t_{comb_min} ≥ t_{hold}. Notice that hold time is independent of clock frequency!'}, {'number': '8.3', 'title': 'Shift Register Counters: Ring vs Johnson', 'content': '• <b>For an n-flip-flop register:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Binary Counter:</b> Has <b>2^n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Ring Counter:</b> Output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> Inverted output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>2n</b> unique states!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An n-flip-flop Johnson counter has 2n states, while an n-flip-flop Ring counter has only n states! Unused states: Ring has (2^n - n) unused states; Johnson has (2^n - 2n) unused states.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Maximum Clock Frequency', 'problem': 'A sequential circuit has flip-flop t_cq = 2 ns, t_setup = 1.5 ns, and combinational logic delay = 4.5 ns. What is the maximum clock frequency f_max?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for minimum clock period: T_clk ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.', 'Step 2: T_clk_min = 2 ns + 4.5 ns + 1.5 ns = 8 ns.', 'Step 3: Maximum frequency f_max = 1 / T_clk_min = 1 / (8 × 10^(-9) s) = 125 MHz.'], 'answer': 'Maximum clock frequency = 125 MHz.'}], 'exam_traps': ["In negative edge-triggered ripple counter: connecting Q to clock gives an UP counter; connecting Q' gives a DOWN counter!", 'Race-around condition in JK flip-flop occurs when J = 1, K = 1 and clock pulse width t_p > propagation delay t_pd in level-triggered latches.']}}
